                        Chapter 4: Quadratic Equations
                        Key Concepts:
                        • A quadratic equation is an equation of the form ax² + bx + c = 0, where a ≠ 0
                        • The solutions (roots) can be found using the quadratic formula: x = [-b ± √(b²-4ac)] / 2a
                        • Discriminant (Δ) = b² - 4ac determines the nature of roots
                        • If Δ > 0: Two distinct real roots
                        • If Δ = 0: One repeated real root
                        • If Δ < 0: No real roots (imaginary roots)
                        How to Solve:
                        1. Write the equation in standard form ax² + bx + c = 0
                        2. Identify a, b, and c values
                        3. Calculate the discriminant
                        4. Apply the quadratic formula
                        5. Simplify to get the roots
                        Example: Solve x² - 5x + 6 = 0
                        Here a=1, b=-5, c=6
                        Δ = 25 - 24 = 1
                        x = (5 ± 1)/2 = 3 or 2
                    

                    
                        Chapter 1: Real Numbers
                        Key Concepts:
                        • Real numbers include all rational and irrational numbers
                        • Euclid's Division Lemma: For any two positive integers a and b, there exist unique integers q and r such that a = bq + r, where 0 ≤ r < b
                        • HCF (Highest Common Factor) can be found using Euclidean algorithm
                        • Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic: Every composite number can be expressed as a product of prime numbers
                        • For a fraction to have terminating decimal, denominator must have only factors of 2 and 5
                        Important Formulas:
                        • HCF × LCM = Product of two numbers
                        • Prime factorization helps find HCF and LCM
                        Types of Decimals:
                        • Terminating: 1/4 = 0.25
                        • Non-terminating repeating: 1/3 = 0.333...
                        • Non-terminating non-repeating (irrational): π, √2
                    

                    
                        Chapter 6: Triangles
                        Key Concepts:
                        • Similar triangles have the same shape but different sizes
                        • Two triangles are similar if their corresponding angles are equal (AA similarity)
                        • Basic Proportionality Theorem (Thales' Theorem): If a line is drawn parallel to one side of a triangle, it divides the other two sides proportionally
                        • Congruent triangles are identical in shape and size
                        Criteria for Similar Triangles:
                        • AA (Angle-Angle) Similarity
                        • SSS (Side-Side-Side) Similarity
                        • SAS (Side-Angle-Side) Similarity
                        Important Theorems:
                        • Pythagoras Theorem: In a right triangle, a² + b² = c²
                        • Area ratio of similar triangles = (ratio of corresponding sides)²
                    
                
            

            
            
                
