                        Chapter 3: Metals and Non-metals
                        Key Concepts:
                        • Metals are elements that lose electrons to form positive ions (cations)
                        • Non-metals are elements that gain electrons to form negative ions (anions)
                        • Metalloids have properties of both metals and non-metals
                        Properties of Metals:
                        • Lustrous (shiny)
                        • Malleable (can be hammered into sheets)
                        • Ductile (can be drawn into wires)
                        • Good conductors of heat and electricity
                        • Sonorous (produce sound when struck)
                        • Generally solid at room temperature (except mercury)
                        Properties of Non-metals:
                        • Usually not lustrous
                        • Brittle (break easily)
                        • Poor conductors of heat and electricity
                        • Can be solid, liquid, or gas at room temperature
                        Chemical Reactions:
                        • Metal + Oxygen → Metal Oxide
                        • 4Na + O₂ → 2Na₂O (burns with bright flame)
                        • Non-metal + Oxygen → Non-metal Oxide
                        • S + O₂ → SO₂ (burns with blue flame)
                    

                    
                        Chapter 1: Chemical Reactions and Equations
                        Key Concepts:
                        • A chemical reaction is a process where substances are transformed into new substances
                        • Chemical equations represent reactions using symbols and formulas
                        • Law of Conservation of Mass: Matter cannot be created or destroyed, only changed
                        Types of Chemical Reactions:
                        • Combination: A + B → AB (e.g., 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O)
                        • Decomposition: AB → A + B (e.g., 2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂)
                        • Displacement: A + BC → AC + B (e.g., Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu)
                        • Double Displacement: AB + CD → AD + CB
                        Balancing Equations:
                        1. Count atoms on both sides
                        2. Add coefficients to balance
                        3. Never change subscripts
                        4. Check that equation is balanced
                        Indicators of Chemical Reaction:
                        • Change in color
                        • Production of gas
                        • Formation of precipitate
                        • Change in temperature
                    

                    
                        Chapter 5: Periodic Classification of Elements
                        Key Concepts:
                        • Periodic table arranges elements in order of increasing atomic number
                        • Mendeleev's Periodic Law: Properties of elements are periodic functions of their atomic weights
                        • Modern Periodic Law: Properties of elements are periodic functions of their atomic numbers
                        Organization:
                        • Rows are called Periods (1-7)
                        • Columns are called Groups (1-18)
                        • Period number = number of electron shells
                        • Group number = valence electrons
                        Classification:
                        • Metals: Left side (s, d, f blocks)
                        • Non-metals: Right side (p block)
                        • Metalloids: Staircase line
                        Trends:
                        • Atomic size decreases left to right in a period
                        • Atomic size increases top to bottom in a group
                        • Ionization energy increases left to right
                        • Electronegativity increases left to right
                    
                
            

            
            
                
