                        Chapter 1: The French Revolution (1789-1799)
                        Key Concepts:
                        • The French Revolution was a major social and political upheaval in France
                        • It marked the end of feudalism and the beginning of modern political thought
                        • Caused by: Heavy taxation, food shortage, Enlightenment ideas, weak leadership
                        Key Events:
                        • 1789: Storming of Bastille (July 14) - became symbol of revolution
                        • August 1789: Declaration of the Rights of Man and Citizen
                        • October 1789: Women's March on Versailles
                        • 1791: Royal family's failed escape attempt (Flight to Varennes)
                        • 1792: France declared a Republic
                        • 1793: Execution of King Louis XVI
                        • 1793-1794: Reign of Terror under Robespierre
                        Important Figures:
                        • Maximilien Robespierre: Led the Reign of Terror
                        • Georges Danton: Moderate revolutionary leader
                        • Jean-Paul Marat: Radical leader and journalist
                        Outcomes:
                        • End of feudalism and monarchy
                        • Rise of nationalism
                        • Spread of democratic ideals across Europe
                        • Influenced revolutions worldwide
                    

                    
                        Chapter 3: Nazism and the Rise of Hitler
                        Key Concepts:
                        • Nazism was an extreme form of fascism developed in Germany
                        • Adolf Hitler became leader of Nazi Party (NSDAP) in 1921
                        • Nazi ideology: Racial superiority, militarism, total state control
                        Why Did Hitler Rise to Power?
                        • Treaty of Versailles (1919) humiliated Germany with harsh terms
                        • Economic crisis and Great Depression (1929) caused mass unemployment
                        • Political instability of Weimar Republic
                        • Hitler promised to restore German pride and strength
                        • Used propaganda and violence effectively
                        Key Events:
                        • 1923: Failed Munich Putsch (Beer Hall Putsch)
                        • 1933: Hitler appointed Chancellor of Germany
                        • 1933: Enabling Act - gave Hitler dictatorial powers
                        • 1934: Night of the Long Knives - purged rivals
                        • 1935: Nuremberg Laws - stripped Jews of citizenship
                        • 1938: Kristallnacht - violent persecution of Jews
                        Nazi Policies:
                        • Lebensraum (living space) - territorial expansion
                        • Rearmament - built up military power
                        • Persecution of minorities, especially Jews
                        • Total mobilization for war
                        • The Holocaust - systematic genocide of 6 million Jews
                    

                    
                        Chapter 5: Towards Equality (Indian Constitution)
                        Key Concepts:
                        • India's Constitution was adopted on January 26, 1950 (Republic Day)
                        • Dr. Bhimrao Ambedkar was the chief architect of the Constitution
                        • Constitution established India as a sovereign democratic republic
                        Fundamental Rights (Part III):
                        • Right to Equality: No discrimination based on religion, caste, sex, race, place of birth
                        • Right to Freedom: Freedom of speech, assembly, association, movement, residence
                        • Right against Exploitation: Prohibition of slavery and forced labor
                        • Right to Freedom of Religion: Freedom to practice any religion
                        • Right to Constitutional Remedies: Can approach courts for protection of rights
                        Directive Principles of State Policy (Part IV):
                        • Guide the state in making laws and policies
                        • Not directly enforceable but morally binding
                        • Include: Welfare of people, education, healthcare, environment protection
                        Significance:
                        • Largest written constitution in the world
                        • Guarantees social and political equality
                        • Protects rights of minorities and marginalized sections
                        • Has been amended 104 times to keep pace with changing needs
                    
                
            
        

        
